, Engineered Structures, and Forged Wheels. The Engine Products segment offers airfoils and seamless rolled rings primarily for aircraft engines and industrial gas turbines; and rotating and structural parts. The Fastening Systems segment produces aerospace fastening systems, as well as commercial transportation, industrial, and other fasteners; and latches, bearings, fluid fittings, and installation tools. The Engineered Structures segment provides titanium ingots and mill products, aluminum and nickel forgings, and machined components and assemblies for aerospace and defense applications; and titanium forgings, extrusions, and forming and machining services for airframe, wing, aero-engine, and landing gear components. The Forged Wheels segment offers forged aluminum wheels and related products for heavy-duty trucks and commercial transportation markets. The company was formerly known as Arconic Inc. Howmet Aerospace Inc. was founded in 1888 and is based in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.","ceo_bio":"Mr. John C. Plant FCA | Executive Chairman & CEO | Age 71 | Compensation: $8.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 25K people. Revenue per employee of $324K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers. Each employee generates $56K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 26\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Howmet Aerospace Inc. is a large-cap industrials business with a $86.5B market cap on $8.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +13% over the past three years and -10% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 13% against a 10% cost of capital, a 4-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $1.4B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 17 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.25\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 11.6\u00d7.","Shareholder yield is only 1.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.2\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 311% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":3.1104748310434247},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Heavy insider selling (net $11.6M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-11560540.62},{"text":"Significant goodwill (36% of assets) from past acquisitions \u2014 a soft asset a conservative appraisal discounts to zero","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwi